<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Entebbe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Entebbe is a town on a peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> about 35 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>, the seat of the British colonial administration for most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> period. It is the site of Uganda's main international airport, where in July 1976 Israeli commandos rescued the passengers of a hijacked Air <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> airliner held there with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>'s government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/entebbe.jpg" alt="Entebbe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mateo.Mfalme · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entebbe_vue_du_ciel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Entebbe lies on a peninsula on the northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, in what is now Wakiso District. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luganda">Luganda</a> word entebbe, meaning seat or chair. It was probably given because a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Baganda</a> chief sat there to judge legal cases. The site was cultural ground for the Mamba clan, and was called entebbe za Mugula after Mugula, the title of a chief of a subdivision of that clan. The town covers 56.2 square kilometres, of which 20 are water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Entebbe became a British colonial administrative and commercial centre in 1893, when Sir Gerald Portal, a colonial Commissioner, used it as a base. It was the seat of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of Uganda until independence in 1962, and State House, first the residence of British governors, later became the president's office and residence. The National Botanical Gardens were laid out in 1898, and Entebbe Golf Club, established in 1900, is the oldest golf course in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. Kigungu, near the town, is the historical site where the first Catholic missionaries, Brother Amans and Father Lourdel, landed to establish their faith in Uganda.</p><p>Entebbe International Airport has been the scene of events known well beyond Uganda. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from it in 1952 when she learned of her father's death and that she had become queen. On 4 July 1976 soldiers of the Sayeret Matkal, Paratroopers Brigade and Golani Brigade of the Israeli Army freed more than 100 hostages after a hijacking by a group of Palestinian and German militants. This was Operation Entebbe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The airport is Uganda's largest commercial and military airport, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> has replaced Entebbe as the seat of power. The town has a population recorded at 69,958 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2014. It is home to the Uganda Virus Research Institute, a government body whose research centres on infectious diseases such as HIV, tuberculosis and malaria, and to the Uganda Wildlife Education Centre, which also serves as the national zoo, and Nkumba University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Entebbe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July 1976 Israeli commandos freed hostages held at <b>Entebbe</b> airport, humiliating his regime.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Military</a></li></ul></section>
